%U A401529 58118487922600818539669284,550895385060405024736682016555,10317915448022153257436790845301392,382734798824697142019083381632799659471
%N A401529 Number of labeled simple graphs on n vertices (not necessarily connected) with burning number 2.
%H A401529 Anthony Bonato, Jeannette Janssen, and Elham Roshanbin, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/15427951.2015.1103339">How to Burn a Graph</a>, Internet Mathematics, 12(1-2) (2016), 85-100. See Theorem 3.1.
%F A401529 a(1) = 0.
%F A401529 a(n) = 2^binomial(n,2) - A100743(n) for n >= 2.
%F A401529 a(n) = Sum_{s=1..n} (-1)^(s+1)*binomial(n,s)*2^binomial(n-s,2)*Sum_{j=0..floor(s/2)} s!*(n-s+1)^(s-2*j)/(2^j*j!*(s-2*j)!) for n >= 2.
%F A401529 a(n) = T(n,0) + T(n,1) for n >= 2, where T is the triangle in A327366.
%e A401529 For n = 3, all 8 labeled graphs except the edgeless graph have burning number 2, so a(3) = 7.
%e A401529 For n = 4, the graphs not counted have maximum degree at most 1. There are 1 edgeless graph, 6 single-edge graphs, and 3 perfect matchings, so a(4) = 2^6 - (1+6+3) = 54.
%Y A401529 Cf. A006125, A100743, A263322, A327366.
%K A401529 nonn,new
%O A401529 1,2
